HUMAN-4: A Human Cognitive Systems Architecture for Human-AI Collaboration and Hidden Problem Discovery in Moon-to-Mars Infrastructure

A Role-Theoretic, Systems-Engineering, and Experimental Framework for Human-AI Cognitive Councils

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI  : chatGpt, Claude, Gemini, Deepseek, Meta AI

Abstract

This thesis proposes HUMAN-4, a hybrid human-AI cognitive architecture comprising four specialized Human Cognitive Systems Agents (HCSAs)—formally defined roles with distinct epistemic responsibilities, decision rights, and AI interaction protocols—collaborating with artificial intelligence systems to discover, analyze, and resolve cross-domain problems in Moon-to-Mars infrastructure development.

The central hypothesis is that structured human cognitive specialization, when formally integrated with AI systems through defined interaction protocols and accountability structures, produces measurable improvements in Hidden Problem Discovery (HPD)—the identification of non-obvious dependencies, failure modes, and second-order effects in coupled socio-technical systems—compared to both undifferentiated human teams and AI-only baselines, while maintaining human epistemic sovereignty.

The four functional roles are:

· Systems Integration Agent (SIA): civilization-scale systems coupling, infrastructure interdependencies, resource allocation
· Environmental Constraint Agent (ECA): planetary science, biological adaptation, environmental limits
· Implementation Feasibility Agent (IFA): physical systems, robotics, manufacturing, energy, technological realization
· Governance & Resilience Agent (GRA): ethics, human factors, governance, safety, long-term societal stability

Brain-computer interfaces (BCIs) are explicitly excluded from the core thesis claims. The thesis investigates BCI-mediated interaction as a future extension, subject to independent scientific and ethical validation. The core architecture uses conventional multimodal interfaces (keyboard, voice, visual, adaptive UI) as the baseline, with BCI treated as a separate research question.

The thesis contributes six distinct advances:

1. Human Cognitive Systems Engineering (HCSE): A formal framework treating humans as first-class cognitive agents with specified epistemic responsibilities, decision rights, interaction protocols, failure modes, and performance metrics—extending beyond the "user" paradigm in human-AI interaction research.
2. Four-Dimensional Epistemic Coverage Model: A testable hypothesis that four irreducible constraint dimensions (Systemic Coupling, Environmental Feasibility, Technological Realizability, Human Viability) provide sufficient epistemic coverage for coupled infrastructure problem discovery.
3. Hidden Problem Discovery Benchmark: A reproducible experimental task with multi-stage ground truth validation, penalized discovery metrics, and novel problem detection procedures.
4. Human-AI Cognitive Council Architecture: An adversarial-collaborative framework where four specialized human agents and an AI system engage in structured cross-examination to surface hidden dependencies.
5. Adversarial Cognitive Loop: A formal specification of the Observe-Interpret-Hypothesize-Challenge-Simulate-Evaluate-Decide cycle that operationalizes AI as a hypothesis-space expander rather than an answer provider.
6. Human Epistemic Sovereignty Protocol Suite: Anti-deskilling protocols, trust calibration mechanisms, AI abstention rules, and authority preservation procedures for long-horizon human-AI collaboration.

The thesis is falsifiable: it specifies hypotheses with operational definitions, null hypotheses, and pre-registered falsification criteria. It is scoped: it focuses on one simulation environment, one task class, and controlled comparisons, leaving broader claims for future work.

