Chapter 1: Formal Problem Statement

1.1 The Multiplanetary Systems Challenge

The transition toward permanent human activity beyond Earth constitutes a class of problems characterized by deep subsystem coupling, long temporal horizons, and irreversible failure modes. NASA's Moon-to-Mars Architecture Definition Document (Revision C, 2025) explicitly frames exploration as an evolving system-of-systems involving human systems, autonomous systems, communications, habitation, logistics, mobility, power, transportation, infrastructure, data systems, and in-situ resource utilization (ISRU). The European Space Agency's Terrae Novae 2030+ Strategy Roadmap similarly treats lunar and Martian development as coupled infrastructure programs requiring integrated systems engineering and model-based systems engineering (MBSE) to maintain architectural coherence.

Definition 1.1 (Coupled Infrastructure System). A coupled infrastructure system $\mathcal{S}$ is a tuple:

\mathcal{S} = \langle \mathcal{C}, \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{D}, \mathcal{F}, \mathcal{X}, \mathcal{U} \rangle

where:

· $\mathcal{C} = \{C_1, C_2, \ldots, C_n\}$ is a finite set of subsystems (components)
· $\mathcal{E} \subseteq \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{C}$ is a set of directed edges representing dependency relations
· $\mathcal{D}: \mathcal{E} \to \mathbb{R}^+$ assigns to each dependency edge a propagation delay (time constant)
· $\mathcal{F}: \mathcal{E} \times \mathbb{R}^+ \to \mathbb{R}^+$ assigns to each edge a coupling strength function (potentially nonlinear, time-varying)
· $\mathcal{X}(t) \in \Omega \subseteq \mathbb{R}^N$ is the full system state vector with $N = \sum_{k=1}^{4} n_k$ dimensions partitioned into four domain sub-vectors
· $\mathbf{u}(t) \in U_{\text{admissible}} \subset \mathbb{R}^m$ is the admissible control input vector

Definition 1.2 (Failure Propagation). Given a failure at component $C_i$ at time $t_0$, the failure propagates to component $C_j$ if there exists a directed path $P = (C_i = C_{k_0}, C_{k_1}, \ldots, C_{k_m} = C_j)$ in $\mathcal{S}$ such that for each edge $(C_{k_\ell}, C_{k_{\ell+1}}) \in P$:

\int_{t_0}^{t_0 + \tau} \mathcal{F}((C_{k_\ell}, C_{k_{\ell+1}}), t - t_0) \, dt \geq \theta_{k_{\ell+1}}

where $\theta_{k_{\ell+1}}$ is the failure threshold for component $C_{k_{\ell+1}}$ and $\tau = \sum_{\ell=0}^{m-1} \mathcal{D}((C_{k_\ell}, C_{k_{\ell+1}}))$.

This formalization captures the essential difficulty: failures propagate along directed paths through coupled infrastructure, with time delays and coupling strengths that may be unknown to system operators. The canonical example from NASA's ISRU technology assessments is the energy-water-food-agriculture-population-health-industrial production-logistics-economic stability cascade (Do et al., 2016; Owens et al., 2024). A failure in regenerative life support (ECLSS) propagates through ISRU propellant production, which shifts power demands, which constrains habitat thermal management, which affects crew workload, which degrades maintenance quality, which accelerates ECLSS failure—a feedback loop that may take months to manifest.

1.2 System State and Coupling Definitions

Let $\mathcal{S}$ be a coupled socio-technical infrastructure system with state vector $\mathcal{X}(t) \in \Omega \subseteq \mathbb{R}^N$ partitioned into four domain sub-vectors:

\mathcal{X}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{x}_{\text{sys}}(t) \\ \mathbf{x}_{\text{env}}(t) \\ \mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}}(t) \\ \mathbf{x}_{\text{hum}}(t) \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_1 + n_2 + n_3 + n_4}, \quad \sum_{k=1}^4 n_k = N

where:

· $\mathbf{x}_{\text{sys}} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_1}$ represents inter-subsystem network coupling states
· $\mathbf{x}_{\text{env}} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_2}$ represents planetary environmental parameters
· $\mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_3}$ represents physical implementation and degradation states
· $\mathbf{x}_{\text{hum}} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_4}$ represents cognitive, physiological, and organizational human states

The system dynamics are governed by the controlled non-autonomous differential equation:

\dot{\mathcal{X}}(t) = \mathbf{f}(\mathcal{X}(t), \mathbf{u}(t), t), \quad \mathbf{u}(t) \in U_{\text{admissible}} \subset \mathbb{R}^m

The system Jacobian matrix $J(\mathcal{X}) = \nabla_{\mathcal{X}} \mathbf{f}(\mathcal{X}, \mathbf{u}, t) \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N}$ is partitioned into block Jacobians:

J(\mathcal{X}) = \begin{bmatrix} J_{11} & J_{12} & J_{13} & J_{14} \\ J_{21} & J_{22} & J_{23} & J_{24} \\ J_{31} & J_{32} & J_{33} & J_{34} \\ J_{41} & J_{42} & J_{43} & J_{44} \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{sys}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{sys}}} & \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{sys}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{env}}} & \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{sys}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}}} & \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{sys}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{hum}}} \\ \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{env}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{sys}}} & \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{env}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{env}}} & \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{env}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}}} & \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{env}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{hum}}} \\ \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{tech}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{sys}}} & \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{tech}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{env}}} & \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{tech}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}}} & \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{tech}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{hum}}} \\ \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{hum}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{sys}}} & \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{hum}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{env}}} & \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{hum}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}}} & \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{hum}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{hum}}} \end{bmatrix}

The viable state domain $\Omega^* \subset \Omega$ is defined as the intersection of four closed constraint manifolds:

\Omega^* = \bigcap_{k=1}^4 \mathcal{K}_k = \left\{ \mathcal{X} \in \Omega \;\middle\vert\; \mathbf{g}_k(\mathcal{X}) \le \mathbf{0}, \; k \in \{1, 2, 3, 4\} \right\}

where each $\mathbf{g}_k: \Omega \to \mathbb{R}^{p_k}$ is a vector-valued constraint function defining the boundary of a distinct constraint dimension.

1.3 The Hidden Problem Discovery Problem

Definition 1.3 (Hidden Problem). A hidden problem $p$ in a coupled infrastructure system $\mathcal{S}$ is a tuple:

p = \langle \mathcal{P}_p, \mathcal{T}_p, \mathcal{I}_p, \mathcal{O}_p \rangle

where:

· $\mathcal{P}_p$ is a failure propagation path in $\mathcal{S}$ (a directed path in the dependency graph)
· $\mathcal{T}_p$ is the characteristic timescale of the failure propagation
· $\mathcal{I}_p$ is the set of initial conditions that trigger the failure
· $\mathcal{O}_p$ is the observable signature of the failure (the set of telemetry signals that indicate the failure is occurring)

A hidden problem is "hidden" precisely because $\mathcal{P}_p$ is not immediately evident from the structure of $\mathcal{S}$ as represented in system documentation, and $\mathcal{T}_p$ is sufficiently long that the failure is not detected during the design or initial operation phases.

Definition 1.4 (Hidden Problem Discovery Task). Given a coupled infrastructure system $\mathcal{S}$, a set of initial conditions $\mathcal{I}$, and a time horizon $T$, the Hidden Problem Discovery (HPD) task requires an agent (human, AI, or hybrid team) to identify the set of hidden problems $\mathcal{P}^*$ that will manifest within time $T$ under conditions $\mathcal{I}$.

Definition 1.5 (Hidden Dependency Detection Rate). Let $\mathcal{P}^*$ be the ground-truth set of hidden problems in a scenario, and let $\hat{\mathcal{P}}$ be the set of hidden problems identified by an agent. The Hidden Dependency Detection Rate (HDDR) is:

\text{HDDR} = \frac{|\hat{\mathcal{P}} \cap \mathcal{P}^*|}{|\mathcal{P}^*|}

Definition 1.6 (Penalized Problem Discovery Score). To address the limitation that HDDR does not penalize false discoveries or reward novel valid discoveries, we define the Penalized Problem Discovery Score ($\text{HPD-}F_1$):

\text{HPD-}F_1 = \frac{2 \cdot |\hat{\mathcal{P}} \cap \mathcal{P}^*| + w_N \cdot |\hat{\mathcal{P}}_N|}{|\hat{\mathcal{P}}| + |\mathcal{P}^*|}

where:

· $\hat{\mathcal{P}}_N$ is the set of novel valid discoveries (problems not in $\mathcal{P}^*$ but validated post-hoc by experts)
· $w_N \in [0, 1]$ is a pre-registered weight for novel discoveries (default: $w_N = 0.5$)
· False discoveries are implicitly penalized through the denominator term $|\hat{\mathcal{P}}|$

The central research question is: What cognitive architecture maximizes HPD performance for Moon-to-Mars infrastructure systems while maintaining human epistemic sovereignty?

1.4 The Limits of Current Approaches

Autonomous AI optimization offers computational capacity for scenario analysis, dependency mapping, and optimization across high-dimensional spaces. However, AI systems exhibit known failure modes:

· Objective misspecification: optimizing for incorrect or incomplete objectives (Amodei et al., 2016; Krakovna et al., 2020)
· Distribution shift: failure when deployed outside training conditions (Hendrycks et al., 2021)
· Context blindness: inability to recognize contextual constraints obvious to embodied humans (Marcus, 2020)
· Confident error: producing errors that are difficult to detect without domain expertise (Lin et al., 2022)

Human expert teams bring contextual understanding, physical intuition, embodied experience, values, creativity, and ethical judgment (Kahneman & Klein, 2009; Klein, 2017). However, individual humans cannot maintain mental models of coupled systems at civilization scale, and teams face coordination failures, communication overhead, and cognitive biases (Salas et al., 2008; Woolley et al., 2010).

Recent human-AI teaming research documents a negative synergy paradox: human-AI teams often underperform AI alone on judgment tasks when humans override correct AI recommendations, when AI recommendations induce automation bias, or when cognitive load prevents effective oversight (Vodrahalli et al., 2022; Chiang et al., 2024; Lee et al., 2025).

1.5 The Gap: Human Roles Are Under-Formalized

A significant gap exists in current human-AI teaming research: human agents are poorly formalized compared to their AI counterparts. AI agents have explicit architectures, memory systems, tool access, planning mechanisms, objectives, and evaluation metrics. Human experts are typically treated as "users" whose cognitive characteristics are assumed rather than specified (Bradshaw et al., 2013; Johnson et al., 2023).

This thesis addresses this gap by proposing that specialized humans can be formally modeled as first-class cognitive agents with:

· Epistemic responsibilities: what they are accountable for knowing
· Decision rights: what they are authorized to decide
· Interaction protocols: how they exchange information with other agents and AI
· Failure modes: how their cognitive biases manifest in this role
· Performance metrics: how their contribution to team performance is measured

This is not a claim that humans are software agents. It is a claim that role-theoretic formalization—borrowed from organizational science and distributed cognition research—can improve human-AI team design by making human contributions explicit, measurable, and testable (Hutchins, 1995; Salas et al., 2012; Klein et al., 2017).

The broader theoretical contribution is Human Cognitive Systems Engineering (HCSE)—the systematic design of human roles, interaction protocols, and cognitive interfaces within human-AI systems, treating human cognition as a designable component of the overall system architecture.

1.6 Scope and Boundaries

This thesis makes specific, falsifiable claims about human-AI collaboration in Moon-to-Mars infrastructure planning. The experimental program focuses on:

· One simulation environment: A computational digital twin of a lunar/Martian surface infrastructure system with coupled power, ECLSS, ISRU, and crew workload dynamics
· One task class: Hidden Problem Discovery (HPD)—identifying non-obvious dependencies, failure modes, and second-order effects
· Five comparison conditions: Human-only, AI-only (single agent), AI-only (multi-agent), Undifferentiated+AI, and HCSA+AI
· One metric suite: HPD-$F_1$, Problem Discovery Time, Solution Quality, Decision Robustness, Cross-Domain Integration, Cognitive Load, Calibration, Skill Retention

