Chapter 2: Mathematical Foundations

2.1 Role-Theoretic Formalization of Human Cognitive Systems Agents

The four Human Cognitive Systems Agents (HCSAs) are defined using a role-theoretic model borrowed from organizational science and distributed cognition research (Katz & Kahn, 1978; Biddle, 1986; Hutchins, 1995).

Definition 2.1 (Human Cognitive Systems Agent). An HCSA is formally defined as a tuple:

\text{HCSA} = \langle \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{D}, \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{F}, \mathcal{M}, \mathcal{A} \rangle

where:

· $\mathcal{R}$ is the role designation (the functional scope of authority)
· $\mathcal{E}$ is the set of epistemic responsibilities (domains of knowledge the agent is accountable for maintaining)
· $\mathcal{D}$ is the set of decision rights (classes of decisions the agent is authorized to make unilaterally, jointly, or recommend)
· $\mathcal{I}$ is the set of interaction protocols (specified procedures for information exchange)
· $\mathcal{F}$ is the set of failure modes (known cognitive biases and performance limitations associated with this role)
· $\mathcal{M}$ is the set of performance metrics (measurable indicators used to evaluate contribution)
· $\mathcal{A}$ is the AI interface specification (how the agent interacts with AI tools)

This formalization does not claim that humans have clean utility functions or can be modeled like software agents. It claims that specifying role responsibilities, authority boundaries, and interaction protocols improves team coordination and reduces ambiguity—a finding well-established in organizational science (Salas et al., 2012; Mathieu et al., 2017).

2.2 The Four Constraint Dimensions: A Proposition

The four-role structure maps to irreducible dimensions of constraint that any coupled infrastructure system must satisfy. We derive these dimensions from a formal analysis of socio-technical system requirements, drawing on the constraint-based framework of Vicente (1999) and the resilience engineering tradition of Hollnagel et al. (2006).

Definition 2.2 (System Constraint Dimension). A system constraint dimension is a class of requirements that (a) must be satisfied for system viability, (b) cannot be derived from other constraint classes, and (c) generates distinct failure modes when violated.

Proposition 2.1 (Irreducibility of Four Constraint Dimensions). Let $\mathcal{S}$ be a coupled socio-technical infrastructure system operating in a planetary environment $\mathcal{V}$. The feasible state domain $\Omega^*$ cannot be represented by fewer than four constraint sets $\{\mathcal{K}_1, \mathcal{K}_2, \mathcal{K}_3, \mathcal{K}_4\}$, as each boundary condition is functionally, structurally, and algebraically non-subsumable.

Proof. To establish irreducibility, we demonstrate that no constraint manifold $\mathcal{K}_i$ can be expressed as a coordinate transformation or algebraic combination of the remaining manifolds $\mathcal{K}_{j \neq i}$.

Systemic Coupling Boundary ($\mathcal{K}_1$): Suppose $\mathcal{K}_1$ is decomposable into isolated subsystem boundaries such that $J_{11}$ is block-diagonal. Because $\mathcal{S}$ contains coupled infrastructure dependencies, there exists at least one non-vanishing off-diagonal element:

\exists \, (i, j), i \neq j \quad \text{such that} \quad J_{11, ij} = \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{sys}, i}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{sys}, j}} \neq 0

Furthermore, cross-domain coupling yields non-zero Jacobians $J_{12}, J_{13}, J_{14} \neq \mathbf{0}$. Therefore, the objective performance functional:

\mathcal{J}(\mathcal{X}) = \int_0^T L(\mathcal{X}, \mathbf{u}) \, dt

exhibits non-separable state dynamics:

\frac{\partial^2 L}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{sys}} \partial \mathbf{x}_k} \neq \mathbf{0} \quad \forall k \in \{\text{env}, \text{tech}, \text{hum}\}

Systemic viability requires maintaining trajectories where the spectral radius $\rho(J_{11}) < 1$ under perturbation. This structural stability condition depends strictly on off-diagonal coupling terms and cannot be inferred from local subsystem constraints.

Environmental Boundary ($\mathcal{K}_2$): The environmental manifold $\mathcal{K}_2 = \{\mathcal{X} \mid \mathbf{g}_2(\mathbf{x}_{\text{env}}) \le \mathbf{0}\}$ is governed by exogenous planetary dynamics:

\frac{\partial \mathbf{g}_2}{\partial \mathbf{u}} = \mathbf{0} \quad \text{and} \quad J_{21} = \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_{\text{env}}}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{sys}}} \approx \mathbf{0}

Surface control actions cannot alter solar flux or cosmic radiation regimes. Because $\mathbf{x}_{\text{env}}$ is exogenously constrained and invariant to internal control authority $\mathbf{u}$, $\mathcal{K}_2$ cannot be generated by or mapped from internal state transitions $\mathbf{f}_{\text{sys}}$.

Technological Realizability Boundary ($\mathcal{K}_3$): Let $\tau: \mathbb{R}^{n_3} \to [0, 1]^m$ be a Technology Readiness and Physical Realizability operator. $\mathcal{K}_3$ defines thermodynamic and material limits (e.g., Carnot efficiency bounds $\eta \le 1 - \frac{T_C}{T_H}$ and stress-strain thresholds $\sigma \le \sigma_{\text{yield}}$):

\mathcal{K}_3 = \left\{ \mathcal{X} \;\middle\vert\; \mathbf{g}_3(\mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}}) \le \mathbf{0} \right\}

Even in scenarios where $J_{13} = \mathbf{0}$, $\mathcal{K}_3$ explicitly restricts the admissible control set $U_{\text{admissible}}(\mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}})$. Because physical realizability bounds exist independently of environmental availability ($\mathbf{x}_{\text{env}}$) or systemic topology ($\mathbf{x}_{\text{sys}}$), $\mathcal{K}_3$ cannot be derived from $\mathcal{K}_1$ or $\mathcal{K}_2$.

Human Viability Boundary ($\mathcal{K}_4$): The human constraint set $\mathcal{K}_4 = \{\mathcal{X} \mid \mathbf{g}_4(\mathbf{x}_{\text{hum}}) \le \mathbf{0}\}$ defines non-compensatory biological, cognitive, and normative boundaries (e.g., blood $\text{pCO}_2$ toxicity, cognitive workload ceilings). Unlike physical energy-mass trade-offs in $\mathcal{K}_3$, $\mathcal{K}_4$ exhibits non-smooth lexicographic preferences:

\lim_{\mathbf{x}_{\text{hum}} \to \partial \mathcal{K}_4} \|\nabla_{\mathcal{X}} \mathcal{J}(\mathcal{X})\| = \infty

Because human physiological survival limits cannot be substituted by excess energy or technical capability ($\frac{\partial \mathbf{g}_4}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}}} = \mathbf{0}$), $\mathcal{K}_4$ is orthogonal to $\mathcal{K}_1, \mathcal{K}_2, \mathcal{K}_3$.

Since the gradient vectors $\{\nabla \mathbf{g}_1, \nabla \mathbf{g}_2, \nabla \mathbf{g}_3, \nabla \mathbf{g}_4\}$ are linearly independent across $\partial \Omega^*$, omitting any set reduces the rank of the constraint space. Hence, four dimensions are strictly irreducible. $\blacksquare$

Lemma 2.2 (Minimality of Four Constraint Dimensions). Omission of any single constraint dimension $d \in \{1, 2, 3, 4\}$ expands the admissible solution space to a relaxed manifold $\tilde{\Omega}_d \supset \Omega^*$. Optimization over $\tilde{\Omega}_d$ yields state trajectories $\mathcal{X}(t)$ that exit the viable domain $\Omega^*$ in finite time $t^* < T$, inducing systemic instability.

Proof. Consider the relaxed optimization problem over a proper subset of constraints where dimension $d$ is omitted:

\max_{\mathbf{u}(t)} \int_0^T L(\mathcal{X}(t), \mathbf{u}(t)) \, dt \quad \text{subject to} \quad \mathcal{X}(t) \in \tilde{\Omega}_d = \bigcap_{k \neq d} \mathcal{K}_k

We analyze the four boundary omission cases:

Case $d = 1$ (Omit Systemic Coupling): Setting $J_{ij} = \mathbf{0}$ for $i \neq j$ assumes local subsystem independence. The unconstrained controller optimizes local states $\dot{\mathbf{x}}_i = \mathbf{f}_i(\mathbf{x}_i, \mathbf{u}_i)$. However, the true trajectory evolves under the unmodeled coupling error $\mathbf{e}(t) = \mathcal{X}_{\text{true}}(t) - \mathcal{X}_{\text{model}}(t)$:

\dot{\mathbf{e}}(t) = J_{11} \mathbf{e}(t) + \sum_{j \neq 1} J_{1j} \mathbf{x}_j(t)

By Gronwall's Inequality, if the maximum real eigenvalue $\lambda_{\max}(J_{\text{coupling}}) > 0$:

\|\mathbf{e}(t)\| \ge \|\mathbf{e}(0)\| e^{\lambda_{\max} t} + \frac{C}{\lambda_{\max}} \left( e^{\lambda_{\max} t} - 1 \right)

Thus, $\mathbf{e}(t)$ grows exponentially, causing $\mathcal{X}(t)$ to cross $\partial \mathcal{K}_1$ at:

t^* = \frac{1}{\lambda_{\max}} \ln \left( 1 + \frac{\lambda_{\max} \epsilon_{\text{threshold}}}{C} \right)

Case $d = 2$ (Omit Environmental Constraints): Relaxing $\mathcal{K}_2$ allows trajectories where resource consumption rates exceed environmental replenishment rates $\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{\text{sys, draw}} > \dot{\mathbf{x}}_{\text{env, flux}}$. The system state vector drives ambient reserves to exhaustion ($\mathbf{x}_{\text{env}} \to \mathbf{0}$), causing immediate collapse of dependent life-support and energy subsystems.

Case $d = 3$ (Omit Technological Realizability): Relaxing $\mathcal{K}_3$ permits control inputs $\mathbf{u}(t) \notin U_{\text{admissible}}$. The optimization engine selects theoretically optimal configurations with technology readiness $\tau(\mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}}) < \tau_{\min}$. The physical system fails to execute the control trajectory, producing catastrophic mechanical or electrical breakdown ($\mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}} \to \partial \mathcal{K}_3$).

Case $d = 4$ (Omit Human Viability): Relaxing $\mathcal{K}_4$ optimizes technical throughput ($P_{\text{gen}}$, $M_{\text{prop}}$) at the expense of human physiological/psychological parameters. Trajectories drive crew workload and atmospheric parameters past critical stress thresholds ($\mathbf{x}_{\text{hum}} \notin \mathcal{K}_4$), triggering human operational failure and subsequent unmaintained system collapse.

Because omitting any single dimension $d$ guarantees that the optimal trajectory exits $\Omega^*$ in finite time $t^* < T$, the four-dimensional constraint structure is minimal. $\blacksquare$

Status: Proposition 2.1 and Lemma 2.2 are presented as architectural propositions rather than mathematical theorems. They are treated as theoretical motivations for the four-role architecture, not as axiomatic truths. The minimality claim is explicitly tested as Hypothesis H9 (Section 5.1), and the proposition may be revised or rejected based on experimental evidence.

