2.4 Failure Mode Formalization

Each HCSA role carries characteristic failure modes—cognitive biases and judgment errors that manifest under specific conditions. We formalize these using the framework of bounded rationality (Simon, 1955; Kahneman, 2011).

Definition 2.5 (Failure Mode). A failure mode $f$ for an HCSA is a function:

f: \mathcal{I} \times \mathcal{K} \to \mathcal{O}

where:

· $\mathcal{I}$ is the information available to the agent
· $\mathcal{K}$ is the agent's knowledge state
· $\mathcal{O}$ is the agent's output (decision, recommendation, or analysis)

A failure mode is a systematic deviation from optimal output: $f(i, k) \neq o^*(i, k)$ where $o^*$ is the normatively correct output.

SIA Failure Modes:

· Optimization bias: $\Pr(f_{\text{SIA}} \text{ selects } o \mid o \text{ maximizes measurable metrics}) > \Pr(o \text{ maximizes all metrics including qualitative})$
· Integration overreach: $\Pr(\text{SIA claims authority over domain } d) > 0$ when $d \notin \mathcal{D}_{\text{SIA}}$
· Analysis paralysis: $\Pr(\text{SIA delays decision} > \tau_{\text{crit}}) > \Pr(\text{delay justified by information value})$

ECA Failure Modes:

· Conservatism bias: $\Pr(\text{ECA rejects solution } s) > \Pr(s \text{ violates environmental constraints})$ — systematic overestimation of environmental risk
· Model overconfidence: $\Pr(\text{ECA treats incomplete model as certain}) > 0$ — treating $\mathcal{K}_{\mathcal{V}}$ as exactly known when it is uncertain
· Reactive stance: Underinvestment in exploration of unknown environmental parameters

IFA Failure Modes:

· Feasibility overreach: $\Pr(\text{IFA approves solution with } \tau < \tau_{\min}) > 0$ — underestimation of technical difficulty
· Sunk cost bias: $\Pr(\text{IFA continues pursuing path } p) > \Pr(p \text{ is feasible})$, conditional on prior investment
· Scope neglect: Focusing on component-level feasibility while underweighting system-level integration challenges

GRA Failure Modes:

· Veto overreach: $\Pr(\text{GRA vetoes solution } s) > \Pr(s \text{ violates ethical constraints})$ — systematic overuse of veto authority
· Value imposition: Failure to distinguish fundamental ethical principles from cultural or personal preferences
· Process focus: Overemphasizing procedural legitimacy at the expense of substantive outcomes

2.5 Anti-Deskilling Protocols: Mathematical Motivation

The literature documents that human-AI teams can experience deskilling when AI systems consistently provide correct answers, leading to reduced human engagement with reasoning processes and eventual loss of the ability to detect AI errors (Budzyń et al., 2025; Noy & Zhang, 2023; Lee et al., 2025).

Definition 2.6 (Human Epistemic Sovereignty). Let $R_{\text{human}}(t)$ be the human agent's reasoning capability at time $t$, measured as HPD-$F_1$ in AI-free conditions. Human epistemic sovereignty is maintained if:

\frac{R_{\text{human}}(t)}{R_{\text{human}}(t_0)} \geq 1 - \epsilon

for all $t > t_0$, where $\epsilon$ is a tolerance parameter (set to 0.10 in this thesis, corresponding to the ≥90% retention criterion in H6).

Proposition 2.3 (Deskilling Risk). Under a naive AI interaction protocol where the AI system provides correct answers without requiring human explanation, $\lim_{t \to \infty} R_{\text{human}}(t) = 0$ in the worst case.

Proof sketch. The human's reasoning capability follows a learning dynamics:

\frac{dR_{\text{human}}}{dt} = \alpha \cdot \text{engagement}(t) - \beta \cdot \text{atrophy}(t)

where $\alpha$ is the learning rate from active engagement and $\beta$ is the atrophy rate from disuse. In a naive protocol where the AI provides answers directly, engagement decreases over time as the human learns to defer to the AI:

\text{engagement}(t) \to 0 \quad \text{as} \quad t \to \infty

leading to:

\frac{dR_{\text{human}}}{dt} \to -\beta R_{\text{human}}

which implies exponential decay: $R_{\text{human}}(t) \to 0$. $\square$

The anti-deskilling protocols (Section 3.5) are designed to maintain engagement above the atrophy threshold by requiring active human reasoning, explanation, and periodic AI-free operation.

