3.2 The Cognitive Loop: Mathematical Specification

The central cognitive loop of HUMAN-4 is:

\boxed{ OBSERVE \rightarrow INTERPRET \rightarrow HYPOTHESIZE \rightarrow CROSS\text{-}EXAMINE \rightarrow SIMULATE \rightarrow EVALUATE \rightarrow DECIDE \rightarrow ACT \rightarrow MEASURE \rightarrow LEARN }

This loop is a synthesis of the OODA Loop (Observe-Orient-Decide-Act) from military decision-making (Boyd, 1987), double-loop learning from organizational theory (Argyris & Schön, 1978), and predictive processing from cognitive science (Clark, 2013).

The novel contributions are the CROSS-EXAMINE and CHALLENGE steps: each human agent can challenge the reasoning of the other three, and the AI system is explicitly tasked with adversarial critique—searching for flaws, oversights, and hidden dependencies in the humans' hypotheses before simulation is conducted.

Definition 3.1 (Cognitive Loop State). The state of the cognitive loop at time $t$ is a tuple:

\mathcal{L}(t) = \langle \mathcal{O}(t), \mathcal{I}(t), \mathcal{H}(t), \mathcal{C}(t), \mathcal{S}(t), \mathcal{E}(t), \mathcal{D}(t), \mathcal{A}(t), \mathcal{M}(t), \mathcal{K}(t) \rangle

where:

· $\mathcal{O}(t)$: observations from the digital twin (telemetry data)
· $\mathcal{I}(t)$: interpretations of observations (what the team believes is happening)
· $\mathcal{H}(t)$: hypotheses about hidden problems (candidate discoveries)
· $\mathcal{C}(t)$: challenges to hypotheses (from AI adversarial critique and cross-examination)
· $\mathcal{S}(t)$: simulation results (outcomes of testing hypotheses)
· $\mathcal{E}(t)$: evaluation of simulation results against criteria
· $\mathcal{D}(t)$: decisions made
· $\mathcal{A}(t)$: actions taken in the digital twin
· $\mathcal{M}(t)$: measurements of outcomes
· $\mathcal{K}(t)$: knowledge state (updated based on learning)

3.3 AI Mode-Switching Specification

The AI system operates in three modes:

Definition 3.2 (Adversarial Critique Mode). In this mode (default during hypothesis generation and solution development), the AI challenges human reasoning, identifies overlooked factors, and proposes alternative hypotheses. The AI's output is a set of challenges $\mathcal{C}(t)$ designed to surface hidden problems before they manifest in simulation.

Definition 3.3 (Neutral Analysis Mode). In this mode (default during conflict resolution), the AI provides unbiased evidence for competing claims without advocating for any particular position. The AI's output is a set of analyses $\mathcal{A}(t)$ that support human decision-making without biasing the outcome.

Definition 3.4 (AI Abstention Mode). In this mode (invoked during AI-free retention tests and when human epistemic sovereignty is threatened), the AI system is disabled, and humans must rely on their own reasoning capabilities. This mode operationalizes the anti-deskilling protocol of "scheduled abstinence."

The transition between modes is governed by the conflict resolution protocol: when agents formally flag a disagreement, the AI shifts to Neutral Analysis Mode; when the disagreement is resolved, the AI returns to Adversarial Critique Mode; when a designated AI-free scenario is active, the AI shifts to Abstention Mode.

3.4 The Moon-to-Mars Digital Twin: Technical Specification

The experimental environment is a computational digital twin of a lunar/Martian surface infrastructure system, purpose-built to capture cross-domain coupling and failure propagation characteristics.

Subsystem Model. The digital twin models a surface settlement with six coupled subsystems:

1. Power Generation & Storage: solar arrays, fuel cells, batteries. State variables: $\mathcal{X}_{\text{power}} = \{P_{\text{gen}}, P_{\text{store}}, P_{\text{load}}, \eta_{\text{array}}\}$ where $\eta_{\text{array}}$ is solar array efficiency (subject to dust degradation).
2. Life Support / ECLSS: oxygen generation, CO$_2$ removal, water recycling. State variables: $\mathcal{X}_{\text{ECLSS}} = \{O_2, CO_2, H_2O, \eta_{\text{membrane}}\}$ where $\eta_{\text{membrane}}$ is membrane efficiency.
3. In-Situ Resource Utilization: water ice extraction, regolith processing, propellant production. State variables: $\mathcal{X}_{\text{ISRU}} = \{M_{\text{H2O}}, M_{\text{prop}}, \rho_{\text{extract}}\}$.
4. Habitat & Thermal Management: temperature control, radiation shielding, structural integrity. State variables: $\mathcal{X}_{\text{habitat}} = \{T_{\text{int}}, D_{\text{rad}}, \sigma_{\text{struct}}\}$.
5. Crew & Workload: crew size, health status, task scheduling, cognitive load. State variables: $\mathcal{X}_{\text{crew}} = \{N_{\text{crew}}, H_{\text{crew}}, W_{\text{crew}}\}$.
6. Logistics & Manufacturing: spare parts, maintenance scheduling, additive manufacturing capability. State variables: $\mathcal{X}_{\text{logistics}} = \{S_{\text{parts}}, M_{\text{cap}}, \tau_{\text{schedule}}\}$.

Coupling Model. The subsystems are coupled through explicit dependency equations derived from NASA ISRU assessments and the Do et al. (2016) Mars One analysis. The coupling is specified as a system of coupled differential equations:

\frac{d\mathcal{X}}{dt} = \mathcal{F}(\mathcal{X}, t; \theta)

where $\mathcal{X} = (\mathcal{X}_{\text{power}}, \mathcal{X}_{\text{ECLSS}}, \mathcal{X}_{\text{ISRU}}, \mathcal{X}_{\text{habitat}}, \mathcal{X}_{\text{crew}}, \mathcal{X}_{\text{logistics}})$ is the full state vector and $\theta$ is a parameter vector.

Key coupling equations include:

Power-ECLSS coupling:
\frac{dO_2}{dt} = g_{\text{O2}}(P_{\text{load}}) - c_{\text{crew}} \cdot N_{\text{crew}}

where $g_{\text{O2}}(P_{\text{load}})$ is the oxygen generation rate as a function of available power.

ECLSS-Crew coupling:
\frac{dH_{\text{crew}}}{dt} = -h_{\text{stress}}(CO_2, O_2) + r_{\text{rest}}(W_{\text{crew}})

where $h_{\text{stress}}$ is the health degradation rate due to poor atmospheric conditions and $r_{\text{rest}}$ is recovery rate as a function of workload.

Crew-Logistics coupling:
\frac{dS_{\text{parts}}}{dt} = -c_{\text{maint}}(W_{\text{crew}}) + p_{\text{manufacture}}(M_{\text{cap}})

where maintenance rate depends on crew workload and manufacturing rate depends on additive manufacturing capability.

ISRU-Power coupling:
\frac{d\eta_{\text{array}}}{dt} = -\delta_{\text{dust}}(\rho_{\text{extract}})

where dust generation from ISRU extraction degrades solar array efficiency.

Feedback Loop Example. The ECLSS-Power-Crew loop is:

\text{ECLSS degradation} \to \text{increased crew maintenance} \to \text{reduced manufacturing} \to \text{delayed solar array repair} \to \text{reduced power} \to \text{further ECLSS degradation}

This is a positive feedback loop with time delays that can take months to manifest.

3.5 Failure Injection Library

The digital twin includes a library of latent failure modes that are not visible to participants unless they actively investigate the relevant subsystem. These failure modes include:

· Cross-segment feedback loops: e.g., ECLSS degradation → increased crew maintenance → reduced manufacturing → delayed solar array repair → reduced power → further ECLSS degradation
· Time-delayed failure propagation: e.g., water recycling membrane degradation that manifests as health effects after 90+ days
· Hidden dependencies: e.g., a logistics schedule that assumes spare parts will arrive from Earth on a specific date, but fails to account for launch window constraints
· Second-order effects: e.g., increased ISRU activity generates dust that degrades solar array efficiency

3.6 Ground Truth Generation: Formal Procedure

For each scenario, the simulation includes a predefined list of "hidden problems" that constitute the ground truth for the Hidden Problem Discovery metric. The ground truth is generated through a multi-stage validation procedure:

Stage 1: Expert Generation. Domain experts (NASA-affiliated systems engineers, planetary scientists, and human factors specialists) independently generate candidate hidden problems based on their knowledge of real system failure modes.

Stage 2: Simulation Validation. Each candidate problem is injected into the digital twin to verify that it produces measurable system degradation. Problems that do not produce detectable effects are eliminated. Formally, a problem $p$ is retained if:

\exists t \in [0, T] : \|\mathcal{X}(t) - \mathcal{X}_{\text{baseline}}(t)\| > \epsilon_{\text{detect}}

where $\mathcal{X}_{\text{baseline}}(t)$ is the system state without the injected problem.

Stage 3: Independent Expert Review. A separate panel of experts (blind to the generation process) reviews the validated problems to confirm that they are (a) non-obvious, (b) discoverable with systematic analysis, and (c) require cross-domain reasoning.

Stage 4: Pilot Testing. During the pilot study, participants (using both HCSA and undifferentiated configurations) attempt to discover the ground truth problems. Problems that are either never discovered (too obscure) or always discovered immediately (too obvious) are eliminated or revised.

Stage 5: Differential Discoverability Check. Problems that are discovered by HCSA teams but not by undifferentiated teams are flagged as high-value ground truth items, as they are precisely the problems that test H1.

Stage 6: Final Inclusion Criteria. A problem is included in the final ground truth set only if:

· At least 50% of expert reviewers independently identified it as valid
· It produces measurable system degradation when injected
· It is discovered by between 20% and 80% of pilot study teams (pooled across configurations) within the scenario time limit

Acknowledgment of limitation: The ground truth list is a working approximation of the set of true hidden problems, not the definitive set. Teams may discover real hidden problems that are not in the ground truth list. To address this, the HPD-$F_1$ metric (Definition 1.6) explicitly includes novel valid discoveries as true positives in a weighted manner, preventing teams from being penalized for discovering valid problems beyond the ground truth.

3.7 The AI System: Tool Suite Specification

The AI system is a suite of specialized tools invoked based on task context. All AI tools operate through a common interface that presents analysis results in a standardized format, highlighting key findings, underlying assumptions, confidence levels, and sources of uncertainty.

Dependency Analyzer. Analyzes the digital twin's coupling network to identify critical paths, bottleneck nodes, and vulnerability points. Formally, given the coupling graph $\mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{E})$ with edge weights $\mathcal{D}$ and coupling strengths $\mathcal{F}$, the analyzer computes:

· Critical paths: paths in $\mathcal{G}$ with highest total coupling strength
· Bottleneck nodes: nodes with highest betweenness centrality
· Vulnerability points: nodes whose failure maximizes system degradation

Scenario Generator. Generates alternative scenarios by perturbing environmental conditions, resource availability, or crew parameters. Used to test robustness of proposed solutions.

Anomaly Detector. Monitors simulation telemetry for early signs of subsystem degradation. Formally, the detector maintains a model of expected telemetry $\hat{\mathcal{T}}(t)$ and flags when:

\|\mathcal{T}(t) - \hat{\mathcal{T}}(t)\| > \epsilon_{\text{anomaly}}

Adversarial Critic. Reviews human hypotheses and proposed solutions, attempting to identify overlooked factors, flawed assumptions, or hidden dependencies. This is the AI tool that most directly operationalizes Hidden Problem Discovery.

Optimization Engine. Performs multi-objective optimization over the simulation parameter space, identifying Pareto-optimal solutions for specified objective functions. Implementation: Multi-objective evolutionary algorithms (NSGA-II, MOEA/D).

Knowledge Retrieval. Provides access to a curated knowledge base of NASA technical reports, ISRU assessments, ECLSS reliability data, and planetary science literature. Implementation: Vector database with embeddings of NASA technical documents, retrieved via semantic search.

AI Compute Budget Specification. To ensure fair comparison between human-AI teams and AI-only systems, a standardized compute budget $B_{AI}$ is defined:

B_{AI} = \langle T_{\text{compute}}, N_{\text{tool calls}}, N_{\text{simulation calls}}, N_{\text{tokens}}, T_{\text{wall-clock}} \rangle

where:

· $T_{\text{compute}}$: total CPU/GPU time available
· $N_{\text{tool calls}}$: maximum number of AI tool invocations
· $N_{\text{simulation calls}}$: maximum number of digital twin simulation runs
· $N_{\text{tokens}}$: maximum token budget for LLM-based tools
· $T_{\text{wall-clock}}$: wall-clock time available

All conditions (human-AI and AI-only) are subject to the same compute budget to ensure a clean comparison of cognitive architecture rather than computational resources.

3.8 Human Epistemic Sovereignty Protocol Suite

The anti-deskilling protocols are operationalized as follows:

Protocol 1: Adversarial AI. The AI's default mode is Adversarial Critique (not answer provision). The AI must challenge human reasoning rather than simply provide solutions.

Protocol 2: Explanatory Burden. When the AI provides analysis or recommendations, the human must articulate (verbally or in writing) the reasoning behind their acceptance or rejection of the AI's output before acting on it.

Protocol 3: Scheduled Abstinence. Designated scenarios (Session 6) are AI-free, requiring humans to rely entirely on their own reasoning. This maintains human epistemic capability and provides a clean measure of skill retention.

Protocol 4: Skill Retention Metrics. Regular AI-free assessments track human reasoning capability over time, enabling early detection of deskilling.

Protocol 5: Explainable Disagreement. When humans reject AI recommendations, they must provide explicit justification. When AI challenges human hypotheses, it must provide explicit reasoning. This ensures that disagreement is epistemically productive rather than arbitrary.

