Chapter 5: Hypotheses and Formal Definitions

5.1 Core Hypotheses

H1: Specialized Team Composition Hypothesis

Four-agent HCSA teams (SIA + ECA + IFA + GRA) will achieve a ≥20% higher Hidden Dependency Detection Rate (HDDR) and ≥15% higher HPD-$F_1$ than undifferentiated four-person expert teams on Hidden Problem Discovery tasks in the Moon-to-Mars digital twin, holding AI access constant.

Null hypothesis: $\mu_{\text{HCSA+AI}} - \mu_{\text{Undiff+AI}} < 0.20 \cdot \mu_{\text{Undiff+AI}}$ for HDDR, and similarly for HPD-$F_1$.

Operationalization: HDDR and HPD-$F_1$ are computed from team-identified hidden problems relative to the ground truth set.

H2: Human-AI Complementarity Hypothesis

Human-AI teams (HCSA+AI) will achieve a ≥15% higher HPD-$F_1$ than AI-only systems (both single-agent and multi-agent AI configurations) on Hidden Problem Discovery tasks, with the human contribution coming from detection of problems that require contextual judgment or value-based reasoning.

Null hypothesis: $\mu_{\text{HCSA+AI}} - \mu_{\text{AI-only}} < 0.15 \cdot \mu_{\text{AI-only}}$ for HPD-$F_1$.

Supplementary analysis: Identify which categories of hidden problems are more or less discoverable by AI alone.

H3: Hidden Problem Discovery Reliability

Hidden Problem Discovery is a reliable construct that can be operationalized and measured in simulation. The reliability framework will assess:

· Inter-scenario consistency: Variance components from mixed-effects models will be estimated, with ICC (Intraclass Correlation Coefficient) ≥ 0.70 indicating acceptable reliability
· Inter-rater agreement: Expert validation of ground truth problems will achieve Fleiss' κ ≥ 0.60

Null hypothesis: ICC < 0.70 or Fleiss' κ < 0.60 (HPD performance is not reliable across scenarios or raters).

H4: Cognitive Diversity Hypothesis

Teams composed of agents with heterogeneous cognitive roles (the four HCSAs) will exhibit greater robustness to scenario variation (measured as Decision Robustness) than homogeneous teams of equal size.

Null hypothesis: Cognitive role diversity does not affect Decision Robustness.

Operationalization: Decision Robustness is measured as performance stability across scenarios with different dominant constraint types.

H9: Minimality of Four Roles (Testable Claim)

Four-agent HCSA teams will achieve equivalent HPD-$F_1$ (within a pre-specified equivalence margin of ±10%) compared to six-agent teams (adding separate Economic Agent and Legal Agent) and eight-agent teams (further adding Public Health Agent and Education Agent), while incurring significantly lower coordination overhead (measured as communication volume and time-to-decision).

Null hypothesis: Additional roles provide significant HPD-$F_1$ improvement beyond the four-agent configuration.

H10: GRA Scope Boundary Condition

The GRA's breadth of epistemic responsibilities will not significantly impair performance relative to a split-role configuration (separate Human Factors Agent and Governance Agent), provided that the GRA receives adequate AI support for knowledge retrieval and analysis.

Null hypothesis: The GRA's broad scope significantly impairs performance compared to a split-role configuration.

H12: AI Compute Budget Control (Methodological)

The AI-only baseline conditions will be constrained by the same compute budget $B_{AI}$ as human-AI teams, ensuring that performance differences are attributable to cognitive architecture rather than computational resources.

Null hypothesis: There is no significant difference in AI tool performance when constrained by the standard compute budget versus unconstrained operation.

5.2 Secondary Hypotheses

H5: Interaction Latency (BCI-Extended)

Under controlled conditions, non-invasive BCI-mediated interaction can reduce the time required to issue commands or retrieve information compared to conventional keyboard/mouse interfaces.

Status: This hypothesis is not tested in the core thesis and is deferred to future work. The threshold for "meaningful improvement" will be established based on a systematic review of current BCI studies (e.g., Willett et al. [2021] report 90 characters per minute for intracortical BCI).

H6: Deskilling Resistance

Teams operating with the anti-deskilling protocols will maintain ≥90% of their baseline AI-free performance on Hidden Problem Discovery tasks after 10+ human-AI collaboration sessions, while teams without these protocols will show significant decline (>15% reduction in AI-free performance).

Null hypothesis: Anti-deskilling protocols do not affect AI-free performance maintenance.

Operationalization: AI-free performance is measured by temporarily removing AI tool access for a designated scenario and comparing team performance to their initial baseline. To address practice effects, a control condition (teams without AI exposure across all sessions) will be included in Study 3.

5.3 Boundary Condition Hypotheses

H7: Task Complexity Threshold

The advantage of the four-HCSA architecture over undifferentiated teams is larger for high-complexity scenarios (many hidden problems, deep cross-domain coupling) than for low-complexity scenarios, with the interaction effect having a medium effect size or greater (d ≥ 0.50).

Null hypothesis: The effect of team composition is constant across scenario complexity.

H8: Communication Overhead

The four-HCSA architecture incurs communication overhead costs that scale with scenario time pressure, such that the architecture's advantage over undifferentiated teams diminishes under severe time constraints (<30 minutes per scenario).

Null hypothesis: Time pressure does not moderate the effect of team composition.

Floor Effect Pre-Registration: If 30-minute scenarios produce HPD-$F_1$ < 0.10 for all conditions (floor effect), the time pressure manipulation is deemed invalid, and H8 is deferred to future work.

