Chapter 6: Experimental Design and Technical Specification

6.1 Overview

The experimental program consists of three phases:

Phase 1: Pilot Study (N = 8 teams)

A small pilot study to validate the digital twin, ground truth problems, AI tool interface, and conflict resolution protocol. Pilot teams use both HCSA and undifferentiated configurations (4 teams each). Results inform the main study design and provide effect size estimates for power analysis.

Phase 2: Study 1 — Computational Simulation (no human subjects)

Compares AI-only configurations (single-agent AI-1, multi-agent AI-M, and AI-HCSA with simulated roles) on the Hidden Problem Discovery task. This establishes the baseline for AI performance and demonstrates that the digital twin and HPD metric are functional before human experiments begin.

Phase 3: Study 2 — Human-in-the-Loop Experiment (human subjects)

Compares four conditions: Human-only, Undifferentiated+AI, HCSA+AI, and (from Study 1) AI-only. This is the primary empirical test of the thesis hypotheses.

Phase 4: Study 3 — Deskilling Resistance Experiment (human subjects)

Compares HCSA+AI teams with and without anti-deskilling protocols, plus a Human-only control condition with no AI exposure, across 10+ sessions to test H6.

6.2 Sample Size and Power Analysis

Study 2 Design: 5 Conditions

The five conditions are:

1. Human-only: Four-person teams, no AI tools
2. AI-1: Single AI agent operating autonomously
3. AI-M: Multiple homogeneous AI agents operating autonomously
4. Undifferentiated+AI: Four-person teams without assigned roles, with AI tools
5. HCSA+AI: Four-person teams with assigned HCSA roles, with AI tools

Proposed sample: N = 12 teams per condition (48 human teams + 24 AI runs = 60 total observation units)

Power analysis: With 12 teams per condition, the study is powered to detect d = 0.80 (large effect) at α = 0.05 and power = 0.80 for pairwise comparisons.

Sequential analysis plan: If the interim analysis at N = 8 teams per condition shows an effect size of d ≥ 0.60, continue to full enrollment; otherwise, recruit additional teams (up to 16 per condition, N = 48 teams total) based on updated power calculations.

Acknowledged limitation: The study is powered for large effects, not medium effects. The minimum hypothesized effects (≥20% HDDR improvement in H1, ≥15% HPD-$F_1$ improvement in H2) may correspond to medium effects (d = 0.50-0.60) that the study may not detect with adequate power. This limitation will be explicitly reported, and null results will be interpreted with appropriate caution.

6.3 Participants

Recruitment. Participants are recruited from graduate programs in relevant disciplines (systems engineering, planetary science, mechanical/electrical engineering, human factors/psychology, ethics/governance). Screening criteria include:

· Domain expertise (≥2 years graduate training or professional experience in relevant field)
· Demonstrated systems thinking ability (assessed via Systems Thinking Scale, Dolansky et al., 2019)
· No prior exposure to the specific simulation scenarios

Team assignment. Participants are assigned to teams using stratified random assignment to balance domain expertise across conditions. Each four-person team in the HCSA condition includes one participant for each of the four roles (SIA, ECA, IFA, GRA), matched to their domain expertise. Teams in the undifferentiated condition include the same mix of domain expertise without role assignment.

Undifferentiated team protocol. Undifferentiated teams are instructed to collaborate as equals, with no assigned roles and no explicit division of responsibilities. To measure informal role differentiation, a post-scenario survey asks each team member to rate the degree to which each member (including themselves) specialized in particular domains during the scenario. Additionally, transcript analysis using Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) quantifies role drift—the degree to which team members spontaneously specialize.

6.4 Simulation Scenarios: Technical Specification

Scenario generation. Twelve scenarios are developed by domain experts, each containing 5-10 ground-truth hidden problems. Scenarios vary along three dimensions:

· Dominant constraint type: systemic coupling, environmental, technological, human viability
· Complexity: number of hidden problems, depth of cross-domain coupling
· Time pressure: scenario time limit: 30 minutes, 60 minutes, or 120 minutes

Scenario validation. Each scenario is validated through the multi-stage ground truth procedure described in Section 3.6, supplemented by independent expert review to confirm that ground-truth hidden problems are non-obvious but discoverable with systematic analysis, require cross-domain reasoning, and are tractable within the time limit.

6.5 Procedure

Session 1: Training. All participants receive training on the simulation environment, the AI tools, and (for HCSA condition) their role specifications. Training continues until participants demonstrate proficiency on a practice scenario. Baseline AI-free performance is measured during this session.

Sessions 2-5: Experimental Scenarios. Teams complete four scenarios, one per session, with order counterbalanced across teams. Scenarios are selected to vary in dominant constraint type and complexity.

Session 6: AI-Free Retention Test. All teams complete one scenario without AI tool access, matched in complexity to the training scenario. Performance is compared to the training baseline to measure skill retention.

Session 7: Debriefing. Teams participate in structured debriefing to gather qualitative data on collaboration processes, perceived challenges, and decision-making strategies.

6.6 Measures: Technical Specification

Primary measures:

· Hidden Dependency Detection Rate (HDDR): proportion of ground-truth problems correctly identified
· HPD-$F_1$: penalized problem discovery score (Definition 1.6)
· Problem Discovery Time (PDT): time from scenario start to correct identification of each problem
· Solution Quality Score (SQS): expert rating of proposed solutions on feasibility, effectiveness, and robustness (1-7 scale)
· Decision Robustness (DR): performance under scenario perturbation (e.g., introducing a surprise event mid-scenario)

Secondary measures:

· Cross-Domain Integration Score (CDIS): expert rating of the degree to which proposed solutions integrate multiple constraint dimensions
· Cognitive Load (CL): NASA-TLX self-report after each scenario, supplemented by real-time physiological measures (pupil dilation, heart rate variability) where feasible
· Calibration (CAL): relationship between team confidence and actual performance
· Team Cohesion: post-scenario survey measuring perceived team coordination and trust
· Skill Retention: comparison of AI-free performance at Session 6 vs. training baseline
· Informal Role Differentiation: post-scenario survey and LDA transcript analysis measuring the degree to which undifferentiated team members specialized in particular domains

Process measures (for mediation analysis):

· Trust Calibration: post-scenario survey measuring the degree to which team trust in AI recommendations matched AI reliability
· Shared Mental Model Quality: post-scenario survey measuring the degree to which team members agreed on system state, constraints, and priorities
· Conflict Resolution Efficiency: proportion of formal disagreements resolved without escalation (from interaction logs)
· Communication Volume: total words exchanged per scenario (from interaction transcripts)

6.7 Analysis Plan

Primary analysis. Mixed-effects models with condition as fixed factor and team as random effect, with HDDR, HPD-$F_1$, PDT, SQS, and DR as dependent variables. Covariates include participant experience, scenario complexity, and scenario order.

Mediation analysis. Test whether cognitive load, trust calibration, shared mental model quality, and conflict resolution efficiency mediate the relationship between team composition and performance. Framed as hypothesis-generating for future research rather than confirmatory, given sample size limitations.

Moderation analysis. Test whether scenario complexity (H7) and time pressure (H8) moderate the effect of team composition on performance.

Multiple testing correction. Family-wise error rates across primary metrics will be controlled at α = 0.05 using the Benjamini-Hochberg False Discovery Rate (FDR) procedure.

Missing data handling. Multiple imputation using chained equations (MICE) for survey missingness < 20%; listwise deletion for survey missingness ≥ 20% with sensitivity analysis.

Outlier detection. Data points with studentized residuals $|r_{ijk}| > 3.5$ in the primary LMM will be investigated for data entry/logging errors. If clean, analyses will be reported both with and without extreme outliers (sensitivity analysis).

Qualitative analysis. Thematic analysis of debriefing transcripts to identify collaboration patterns, conflict resolution strategies, and perceived advantages/disadvantages of each condition.

Pre-registration. The full experimental protocol, hypotheses, analysis plan, and falsification criteria will be pre-registered on OSF (or equivalent) before data collection begins.

6.8 Pilot Study Specification

Pilot participants: 2 teams per configuration (HCSA and undifferentiated), 8 teams total.

Pilot scenarios: 4 scenarios (one per dominant constraint type) with ground truth.

Pilot objectives:

1. Validate the digital twin's ground truth problems (are they discoverable? non-obvious?)
2. Estimate effect sizes for the main study's power analysis
3. Test the conflict resolution protocol
4. Refine the AI tool interface based on cognitive load data
5. Validate the undifferentiated team protocol
6. Check for differential discoverability of ground truth problems across configurations

Pilot success criteria:

· Ground truth problems achieve consensus among participants that they are "non-obvious but discoverable"
· Effect size estimates within the range specified in the hypotheses (d ≥ 0.50)
· Conflict resolution protocol achieves ≥70% resolution rate without escalation
· NASA-TLX scores within acceptable range (mean ≤ 60 on 0-100 scale)
· Differential discoverability analysis identifies problems that are discovered by HCSA teams but not undifferentiated teams

6.9 Limitations

External validity. The simulation is a simplified model of Moon-to-Mars systems. Performance may not transfer to real-world decision-making.

Sample size. Despite the revised design, the study is powered to detect large effects (d ≥ 0.80), not medium effects. The minimum hypothesized effects may correspond to medium effects that the study cannot detect with adequate power.

Participant population. Graduate students may not represent the performance of experienced professionals.

Single task class. Hidden Problem Discovery is one of many tasks required for multiplanetary systems management.

Ground truth approximation. The ground truth list is a working approximation of the true set of hidden problems. The HPD-$F_1$ metric partially addresses this limitation through novel discovery weighting.

Deskilling measurement confound. The AI-free retention test is confounded by practice effects. Study 3 includes a control condition to address this limitation.

Pilot study circularity risk. Ground truth problems are validated by pilot study performance, which may bias the ground truth toward problems that are equally discoverable by all configurations. The differential discoverability check (Stage 5) partially addresses this risk by flagging high-value problems that differentiate configurations.

