Chapter 12: Conclusion

This thesis proposes HUMAN-4, a hybrid human-AI cognitive architecture that formalizes human roles in human-AI teams and tests whether this formalization improves performance on Hidden Problem Discovery—a measurable task that operationalizes the cross-domain reasoning required for multiplanetary infrastructure management.

The architecture is falsifiable: it specifies hypotheses with operational definitions, null hypotheses, and pre-registered falsification criteria. It is scoped: it focuses on one simulation environment, one task class, and controlled comparisons, leaving broader claims for future work. It is grounded: it engages with the existing literature on human-autonomy teaming, joint cognitive systems, team science, and Moon-to-Mars systems engineering.

The mathematical formalization in this version provides a foundation for the thesis claims:

· Proposition 2.1 establishes the irreducibility of four constraint dimensions as a testable architectural proposition (not an axiomatic theorem), justifying the four-role architecture while acknowledging that minimality is tested empirically (H9)
· Definition 1.4 formalizes the Hidden Problem Discovery task
· Definition 1.6 introduces the Penalized Problem Discovery Score (HPD-$F_1$) that addresses false discovery and novel discovery limitations
· Definition 2.5 formalizes failure modes
· Proposition 2.3 establishes the deskilling risk that motivates the anti-deskilling protocols

The central contribution is not the specific number four. It is the broader framework of Human Cognitive Systems Engineering—treating humans as formally designed cognitive roles inside a human-AI system rather than merely as "users." This framework has implications beyond Moon-to-Mars infrastructure, extending to any domain where coupled socio-technical systems require human-AI collaboration for complex problem discovery.

The central question is not "Can we make humans superhuman?" but "Can we engineer a better partnership between human intelligence and artificial intelligence—one that is empirically testable, ethically grounded, and operationally relevant—before civilization becomes too complex for either to manage alone?"

