Title: Human-in-the-Loop Evaluation of Specialized Cognitive Roles (HUMAN-4) vs. Undifferentiated Teams and AI-Only Baselines in Moon-to-Mars Digital Twin Problem Discovery

Pre-Registration Date: Pre-data collection registration (OSF Standard Template)

Authors: Ouadi Maakoul

1. Study & Hypothesis Plan

1.1 Design Overview

Study 2 evaluates Hidden Problem Discovery (HPD) performance in a coupled Moon-to-Mars digital twin across a 5 × 4 mixed factorial design:

Between-Subjects Factor (Teaming Architecture, 5 levels):

1. Human-only: Four-person teams, no AI tools
2. AI-1: Single AI agent operating autonomously
3. AI-M: Multiple homogeneous AI agents operating autonomously
4. Undifferentiated+AI: 4-person expert teams with no assigned roles, with identical AI tool suite
5. HCSA+AI: Specialized 4-role Human Cognitive Systems Agent teams (SIA, ECA, IFA, GRA) with AI tool suite

Within-Subjects Factor (Scenario Dominant Constraint, 4 levels): Systemic Coupling, Environmental Feasibility, Technological Feasibility, Human Viability.

1.2 Primary Hypotheses

H_1: \mu_{\text{HDDR}}(\text{HCSA+AI}) - \mu_{\text{HDDR}}(\text{Undiff+AI}) \ge 0.20 \cdot \mu_{\text{HDDR}}(\text{Undiff+AI})

H_1': \mu_{\text{HPD-}F_1}(\text{HCSA+AI}) - \mu_{\text{HPD-}F_1}(\text{Undiff+AI}) \ge 0.15 \cdot \mu_{\text{HPD-}F_1}(\text{Undiff+AI})

H_2: \mu_{\text{HPD-}F_1}(\text{HCSA+AI}) - \mu_{\text{HPD-}F_1}(\text{AI-only}) \ge 0.15 \cdot \mu_{\text{HPD-}F_1}(\text{AI-only})

H_6: \frac{\text{HPD-}F_{1,\text{Post-AI-Free}}}{\text{HPD-}F_{1,\text{Baseline-AI-Free}}} \ge 0.90 \quad \text{(with anti-deskilling protocols)}

H_7: \beta_{\text{HCSA} \times \text{Complexity}} > 0, \quad d \ge 0.50

H_{11}: \text{Conflict Resolution Rate} \ge 70\%

2. Sampling & Power Plan

Sample Size: N = 12 teams per condition (48 human teams + 24 AI runs = 60 total observation units; N = 96 human subjects across human conditions)

Power Calculation: Powered for a large effect size (d = 0.80, α = 0.05, 1 - β = 0.80) in a linear mixed-effects model.

Sequential Analysis & Stopping Rule: Data collection stops at N = 12 teams/condition if d ≥ 0.60 at interim analysis (N = 8). If d < 0.60, enrollment scales to N = 16 teams/condition (N = 48 total teams).

3. Variables & Operationalization

Independent Variables

· Condition: Categorical (Human-only, AI-1, AI-M, Undifferentiated+AI, HCSA+AI)
· Complexity: Continuous composite score (C_j ∈ [1, 10]) based on graph density, coupling non-linearity, and time-delay magnitudes
· TimePressure: Ordinal factor (30 min, 60 min, 120 min time limits)

Primary Dependent Variables

\text{HDDR}_{ij} = \frac{|\hat{\mathcal{P}}_{ij} \cap \mathcal{P}_j^*|}{|\mathcal{P}_j^*|}

\text{HPD-}F_{1, ij} = \frac{2 \cdot |\hat{\mathcal{P}}_{ij} \cap \mathcal{P}_j^*| + w_N \cdot |\hat{\mathcal{P}}_{N,ij}|}{|\hat{\mathcal{P}}_{ij}| + |\mathcal{P}_j^*|}

where $w_N = 0.5$ (pre-registered novel discovery weight).

· Problem Discovery Time (PDT): Time elapsed (seconds) from scenario start to true-positive identification of problem p_k ∈ \mathcal{P}_j^*
· Solution Quality Score (SQS): Blended 1–7 expert evaluation metric measuring feasibility, safety margins, and efficiency

Secondary & Process Variables

· CognitiveLoad: Subjective NASA-TLX score + mean pupil dilation relative to baseline
· TrustCalibration: Absolute difference between subjective trust score and actual historical AI reliability (|T_{\text{human}} - R_{\text{AI}}|)
· ConflictResolutionRate: Ratio of internally resolved flagged items to total flagged items
· RoleDrift: Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) entropy score on transcript text to measure informal specialization in undifferentiated teams

Covariates

· SystemsThinkingBaseline: Score on the Systems Thinking Scale (Dolansky et al., 2019) collected at Session 1
· DomainExperience: Cumulative years of relevant graduate/professional engineering or scientific training

4. Statistical Analysis Plan

Primary Model: Linear Mixed-Effects (LMM)

Primary hypotheses (H_1, H_2, H_7) will be evaluated using Linear Mixed-Effects Models fitted via Restricted Maximum Likelihood (REML). The primary model for outcome Y_{ijk} (representing HPD-F_1 for team i on scenario j under time pressure k) is:

Y_{ijk} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \text{Condition}_i + \beta_2 \text{Complexity}_j + \beta_3 \text{TimePressure}_k + \beta_4 (\text{Condition}_i \times \text{Complexity}_j) + \beta_5 \text{SystemsThinking}_i + u_{0i} + v_{0j} + \epsilon_{ijk}

where:

· $u_{0i} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_u^2)$ is the random intercept for Team i
· $v_{0j} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_v^2)$ is the random intercept for Scenario j
· $\epsilon_{ijk} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$ is the residual error

Contrast Specifications

· Contrast 1 (H_1): β_1(HCSA+AI) - β_1(Undifferentiated+AI)
· Contrast 2 (H_2): β_1(HCSA+AI) - β_1(AI-only, pooled AI-1 and AI-M)

Multiple Testing Correction

Family-wise error rates across primary metrics (HDDR, HPD-F_1, PDT, SQS) will be controlled at α = 0.05 using the Benjamini-Hochberg False Discovery Rate (FDR) procedure.

5. Exclusion Criteria & Data Cleaning Rules

Technical Invalidation: Any trial experiencing a digital twin outage or network disruption exceeding 15 consecutive minutes will be discarded and rerun with a backup scenario.

Attrition / Non-Completion: Teams failing to complete all 4 main experimental sessions will be excluded entirely from longitudinal analyses (H_6) and replaced.

Manipulation Checks:

· Human condition teams with total internal verbal/written communication volume < 100 words per 60-minute scenario will be flagged for non-engagement and excluded.
· Undifferentiated teams exhibiting formal self-assignment of explicit roles (verified via transcripts) will be excluded from the Undifferentiated baseline.

Outlier Policy: Data points with studentized residual values |r_{ijk}| > 3.5 in the LMM will be investigated for data entry/logging errors. If clean, analyses will be reported both with and without extreme outliers (sensitivity analysis).

Appendices

Appendix A: Full Mathematical Proofs and Formal Derivations

A.1 Proof of Proposition 2.1 (Irreducibility of Four Constraint Dimensions)

We restate Proposition 2.1:

Proposition 2.1. Let $\mathcal{S}$ be a coupled socio-technical infrastructure system operating in a planetary environment $\mathcal{V}$. The feasible state domain $\Omega^*$ cannot be represented by fewer than four constraint sets $\{\mathcal{K}_1, \mathcal{K}_2, \mathcal{K}_3, \mathcal{K}_4\}$, as each boundary condition is functionally, structurally, and algebraically non-subsumable.

Proof. We proceed by demonstrating that each constraint manifold $\mathcal{K}_i$ possesses at least one property that is not expressible as a combination of the other three, and that the gradient vectors of the constraint functions are linearly independent along the boundary $\partial\Omega^*$.

Step 1: Systemic Coupling Boundary ($\mathcal{K}_1$).
The systemic coupling constraint set $\mathcal{K}_1 = \{\mathcal{X}\mid \mathbf{g}_1(\mathcal{X})\le 0\}$ enforces global stability of the coupled dynamics. The key functional property is the non-separability of the performance functional $\mathcal{J}$. Suppose, for contradiction, that $\mathcal{K}_1$ could be derived from $\mathcal{K}_2,\mathcal{K}_3,\mathcal{K}_4$. Then any trajectory $\mathcal{X}(t)$ satisfying the latter three constraints would automatically satisfy the coupled-system stability requirement. However, consider a system where each subsystem individually satisfies its local constraints (environmental, technological, human), but the coupling terms $J_{12},J_{13},J_{14}$ are nonzero and drive the full state vector outside the stability region. Formally, let $\mathbf{u}(t)$ be a control policy that optimizes the separable proxy objective $\sum_{i} w_i f_i(\mathbf{x}_i)$ while ignoring cross-terms. The true dynamics under coupling produce an error $\mathbf{e}(t)$ evolving as:

\dot{\mathbf{e}}(t) = J_{11}\mathbf{e}(t) + \sum_{j\neq 1} J_{1j}\mathbf{x}_j(t).

If $\lambda_{\max}(J_{11})>0$, the error grows unboundedly regardless of how well the individual subsystems meet their own constraints. Hence, systemic stability imposes an independent condition that cannot be inferred from the other three.

Step 2: Environmental Boundary ($\mathcal{K}_2$).
The environmental constraint function $\mathbf{g}_2$ depends only on $\mathbf{x}_{\text{env}}$ and is invariant to control actions: $\partial \mathbf{g}_2/\partial \mathbf{u}=0$. Moreover, the environmental dynamics are exogenous: $J_{21}\approx \mathbf{0}$. No transformation of internal state variables ($\mathbf{x}_{\text{sys}}, \mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}}, \mathbf{x}_{\text{hum}}$) can generate the exogenous planetary limits because these limits are determined by physical laws and initial conditions external to the system design. Therefore, $\mathcal{K}_2$ cannot be represented as a function of $\mathcal{K}_1,\mathcal{K}_3,\mathcal{K}_4$.

Step 3: Technological Realizability Boundary ($\mathcal{K}_3$).
The technological constraint $\mathcal{K}_3 = \{\mathcal{X}\mid \mathbf{g}_3(\mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}})\le 0\}$ restricts the admissible control set $U_{\text{admissible}}$. Even if the environmental and human constraints are satisfied, a solution may be technologically infeasible because $\tau(\mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}})<\tau_{\min}$. This infeasibility is independent of coupling topology ($\mathbf{x}_{\text{sys}}$) and environmental availability ($\mathbf{x}_{\text{env}}$). Formally, $\mathbf{g}_3$ is a function only of $\mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}}$, and its gradient $\nabla_{\mathcal{X}}\mathbf{g}_3$ has nonzero components only in the $\mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}}$ subspace. Hence, $\mathcal{K}_3$ is orthogonal to the other constraint manifolds in the state space.

Step 4: Human Viability Boundary ($\mathcal{K}_4$).
The human viability constraint $\mathcal{K}_4$ involves non-compensatory thresholds: for example, blood $\text{pCO}_2$ above a critical level cannot be offset by increased power or propellant production. The gradient $\nabla_{\mathcal{X}}\mathcal{J}$ diverges as $\mathbf{x}_{\text{hum}}\to\partial\mathcal{K}_4$, reflecting lexicographic preferences. Because $\partial \mathbf{g}_4/\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{tech}}=0$ and $\partial \mathbf{g}_4/\partial \mathbf{x}_{\text{sys}}=0$ (human physiological limits are not traded against technological or systemic variables), $\mathcal{K}_4$ is linearly independent of the others.

Linear Independence of Gradients. On the boundary $\partial\Omega^*$, the active constraint gradients $\nabla\mathbf{g}_i$ are linearly independent. If they were dependent, then there would exist nonzero coefficients $\alpha_i$ such that $\sum_i \alpha_i \nabla\mathbf{g}_i = 0$, implying that one constraint could be expressed as a combination of the others, which we have shown impossible by the above arguments.

Therefore, four constraint dimensions are necessary and irreducible. $\blacksquare$

A.2 Proof of Lemma 2.2 (Minimality of Four Constraint Dimensions)

Lemma 2.2. Omission of any single constraint dimension $d \in \{1,2,3,4\}$ expands the admissible solution space to a relaxed manifold $\tilde{\Omega}_d \supset \Omega^*$. Optimization over $\tilde{\Omega}_d$ yields state trajectories $\mathcal{X}(t)$ that exit the viable domain $\Omega^*$ in finite time $t^*<T$.

Proof. We analyze each omission case separately, following the structure in the main text but with full mathematical rigor.

Case $d=1$ (Omit Systemic Coupling).
Relaxing $\mathcal{K}_1$ means that the optimization problem ignores cross-subsystem interactions. The model used for optimization is:

\dot{\mathbf{x}}_i = \mathbf{f}_i(\mathbf{x}_i,\mathbf{u}_i),\quad i=1,\dots,n,

where each subsystem evolves independently. The true system, however, has coupling terms. Define the coupling error as $\mathbf{e}(t)=\mathbf{x}_{\text{true}}(t)-\mathbf{x}_{\text{model}}(t)$. The error dynamics are:

\dot{\mathbf{e}}(t) = J_{11}\mathbf{e}(t) + \sum_{j\neq 1} J_{1j}\mathbf{x}_j(t).

By the variation of constants formula:

\mathbf{e}(t) = e^{J_{11}t}\mathbf{e}(0) + \int_0^t e^{J_{11}(t-s)}\left(\sum_{j\neq 1} J_{1j}\mathbf{x}_j(s)\right)ds.

If $J_{11}$ has an eigenvalue with positive real part $\lambda_{\max}>0$, then the homogeneous part grows as $e^{\lambda_{\max}t}$. Even if $\lambda_{\max}\le 0$, the integral term may still cause unbounded growth if the coupling inputs $\mathbf{x}_j(s)$ are non-decaying and $J_{11}$ has eigenvalues with zero real part (resonance). In either case, the error will eventually exceed the viability threshold, causing $\mathcal{X}(t)$ to leave $\Omega^*$ at some finite $t^*$. A conservative lower bound for $t^*$ is obtained from the Gronwall inequality as given in the main text:

t^* \ge \frac{1}{\lambda_{\max}}\ln\left(1+\frac{\lambda_{\max}\epsilon_{\text{threshold}}}{C}\right),

where $C$ is a bound on the coupling forcing term. Hence, omission leads to finite-time exit.

Case $d=2$ (Omit Environmental Constraints).
Without $\mathcal{K}_2$, the optimization may extract resources at a rate exceeding planetary replenishment. Let $\mathbf{x}_{\text{env}}$ include a critical resource stock $R(t)$ with dynamics $\dot{R} = -\dot{R}_{\text{draw}}$ (no recharge). The unconstrained optimal draw rate $\dot{R}_{\text{draw}}^*$ may be positive even when $R\to 0$. Since $R(t)$ is monotonically decreasing, there exists $t^*$ such that $R(t^*)=0$, after which dependent subsystems fail. Environmental constraints are thus essential.

Case $d=3$ (Omit Technological Realizability).
Relaxing $\mathcal{K}_3$ permits controls $\mathbf{u}(t)$ that violate physical laws (e.g., exceeding material strength). The optimization will select such controls because they offer better performance in the model. At the moment of execution, the physical system will fail (e.g., structural collapse), causing an immediate jump out of $\Omega^*$. Therefore, $t^*$ is the first time such an infeasible control is applied.

Case $d=4$ (Omit Human Viability).
Without $\mathcal{K}_4$, the optimization may drive $\mathbf{x}_{\text{hum}}$ beyond physiological limits. Consider a crew workload variable $W_{\text{crew}}$ that grows linearly under the optimal control. There exists a threshold $W_{\max}$ beyond which crew performance degrades catastrophically. Since $W_{\text{crew}}(t)$ is monotonically increasing under the optimal policy, $t^*$ exists when $W_{\text{crew}}(t^*)=W_{\max}$.

Thus in all cases, the relaxed optimization produces trajectories that exit $\Omega^*$ in finite time, proving minimality. $\blacksquare$

A.3 Proof of Proposition 2.3 (Deskilling Risk)

Proposition 2.3. Under a naive AI interaction protocol where the AI system provides correct answers without requiring human explanation, $\lim_{t\to\infty} R_{\text{human}}(t)=0$ in the worst case.

Proof. The human reasoning capability $R_{\text{human}}(t)$ evolves according to:

\frac{dR_{\text{human}}}{dt} = \alpha \cdot e(t) - \beta \cdot a(t),

where $e(t)\in[0,1]$ is the engagement level (fraction of time the human actively reasons) and $a(t)\in[0,1]$ is the atrophy level (fraction of time the human relies on AI without reasoning). In a naive protocol, the AI always provides the correct answer; hence, the human learns that deferring to AI minimizes cognitive effort and maximizes performance. Consequently, engagement declines: $e(t)\to 0$ and $a(t)\to 1$ as $t\to\infty$. Thus:

\frac{dR_{\text{human}}}{dt} \to -\beta R_{\text{human}},

which has solution $R_{\text{human}}(t) = R_0 e^{-\beta t}$. Therefore, $\lim_{t\to\infty} R_{\text{human}}(t)=0$. $\blacksquare$

A.4 Additional Formal Derivations

A.4.1 HPD-$F_1$ Score Properties

The Penalized Problem Discovery Score (Definition 1.6) is:

\text{HPD-}F_1 = \frac{2|\hat{\mathcal{P}}\cap\mathcal{P}^*| + w_N|\hat{\mathcal{P}}_N|}{|\hat{\mathcal{P}}| + |\mathcal{P}^*|},

where $w_N\in[0,1]$. We note:

· When there are no novel discoveries and no false positives, $\text{HPD-}F_1 = \text{HDDR}$.
· When $w_N=1$ and all discoveries are novel and valid, the score reduces to $2|\hat{\mathcal{P}}_N|/(|\hat{\mathcal{P}}|+|\mathcal{P}^*|)$, which penalizes over-generation.
· The denominator ensures that false positives reduce the score, as they increase $|\hat{\mathcal{P}}|$ without contributing to the numerator.

A.4.2 Coupling Graph Centrality and Hidden Problems

Let $\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{C},\mathcal{E})$ be the dependency graph with edge weights $\mathcal{D}$ and coupling strengths $\mathcal{F}$. The betweenness centrality of node $v$ is:

C_B(v) = \sum_{s\neq v\neq t} \frac{\sigma_{st}(v)}{\sigma_{st}},

where $\sigma_{st}$ is the number of shortest paths from $s$ to $t$ and $\sigma_{st}(v)$ is the number of those passing through $v$. Nodes with high $C_B$ are likely bottlenecks where hidden failures propagate. The Dependency Analyzer uses this metric to rank potential hidden problem locations.

