Appendix C: Digital Twin Model Equations and Coupling Functions

C.1 Full State Vector and Parameters

The full state vector $\mathcal{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{24}$ (with 6 subsystems, each with 4 state variables) is:

\mathcal{X} = [\mathbf{x}_{\text{power}}^\top, \mathbf{x}_{\text{ECLSS}}^\top, \mathbf{x}_{\text{ISRU}}^\top, \mathbf{x}_{\text{habitat}}^\top, \mathbf{x}_{\text{crew}}^\top, \mathbf{x}_{\text{logistics}}^\top]^\top.

Parameter ranges are derived from NASA ISRU assessments and Do et al. (2016):

Parameter Range Units
Solar array efficiency degradation due to dust $\delta_{\text{dust}}$ 0.001–0.01 day$^{-1}$
ECLSS membrane degradation rate 0.0005–0.005 day$^{-1}$
Crew health recovery rate $r_{\text{rest}}$ 0.05–0.2 day$^{-1}$
Maintenance rate coefficient $c_{\text{maint}}$ 0.01–0.1 day$^{-1}$
Manufacturing rate coefficient $p_{\text{manufacture}}$ 0.02–0.2 day$^{-1}$

C.2 Full Differential Equations

We present the complete system of coupled ODEs used in the digital twin.

Power:

\frac{dP_{\text{gen}}}{dt} = -\delta_{\text{dust}}\rho_{\text{extract}}P_{\text{gen}} + u_{\text{cleaning}}

\frac{dP_{\text{store}}}{dt} = \eta_{\text{charge}}P_{\text{gen}} - \eta_{\text{discharge}}^{-1}P_{\text{load}}

\frac{dP_{\text{load}}}{dt} = \sum_{i} \alpha_i \dot{\mathbf{x}}_i \quad \text{(summed over all subsystems' power demands)}

\frac{d\eta_{\text{array}}}{dt} = -\delta_{\text{dust}}\rho_{\text{extract}}\eta_{\text{array}}

ECLSS:

\frac{dO_2}{dt} = g_{\text{O2}}(P_{\text{load}}) - c_{\text{crew}}N_{\text{crew}}

\frac{dCO_2}{dt} = c_{\text{crew}}N_{\text{crew}} - s_{\text{scrub}}(P_{\text{load}})

\frac{dH_2O}{dt} = r_{\text{recycle}}(P_{\text{load}}) - c_{\text{crew}}N_{\text{crew}}

\frac{d\eta_{\text{membrane}}}{dt} = -k_{\text{foul}}\cdot\text{usage rate}

ISRU:

\frac{dM_{\text{H2O}}}{dt} = -\rho_{\text{extract}}V_{\text{site}}

\frac{dM_{\text{prop}}}{dt} = \eta_{\text{conv}}\rho_{\text{extract}}V_{\text{site}}

\frac{d\rho_{\text{extract}}}{dt} = u_{\text{extract}} - \delta_{\text{dust}}\rho_{\text{extract}}

Habitat:

\frac{dT_{\text{int}}}{dt} = \frac{1}{C_{\text{thermal}}}(Q_{\text{heat}} - Q_{\text{loss}})

\frac{dD_{\text{rad}}}{dt} = \text{radiation flux} - \text{shielding rate}

\frac{d\sigma_{\text{struct}}}{dt} = -\delta_{\text{struct}}\sigma_{\text{struct}}

Crew:

\frac{dN_{\text{crew}}}{dt} = 0 \quad \text{(fixed crew size)}

\frac{dH_{\text{crew}}}{dt} = -h_{\text{stress}}(CO_2,O_2) + r_{\text{rest}}(W_{\text{crew}})

\frac{dW_{\text{crew}}}{dt} = \text{task load} - \text{recovery rate}

Logistics:

\frac{dS_{\text{parts}}}{dt} = -c_{\text{maint}}(W_{\text{crew}}) + p_{\text{manufacture}}(M_{\text{cap}})

\frac{dM_{\text{cap}}}{dt} = \delta_{\text{additive}}M_{\text{cap}}

\frac{d\tau_{\text{schedule}}}{dt} = \text{rescheduling rate}

C.3 Numerical Integration

The system is integrated using a 4th-order Runge-Kutta method with adaptive step size (RK45). The simulation time step is $dt = 0.01$ days for scenario durations up to 180 days. Telemetry is sampled every 0.1 days.

