Appendix F: Error and Gap Analysis

This appendix identifies remaining gaps and errors in the v0.5 document, drawing from the earlier critical review and additional self-analysis. Each item includes severity (High/Medium/Low), description, and recommended fix.

F.1 High Severity

F.1.1 Power and Effect Size Mismatch

· Description: The study is powered for $d=0.80$, but H1 and H2 specify minimum effect sizes of 20% and 15% improvements, which may correspond to medium effects ($d\approx 0.5$–$0.6$). The study may not detect these effects, leading to false negatives.
· Fix: Either (a) increase sample size to achieve power for $d=0.50$ (requires ~64 teams per condition for two-group comparison, which is infeasible), or (b) revise the hypotheses to specify large effects ($d\ge0.80$), or (c) reframe H1/H2 as superiority tests with a pre-specified non-inferiority margin and treat medium effects as exploratory. The most honest approach is (c): pre-register that the study is powered to detect large effects, and if medium effects are observed but not statistically significant, report them as preliminary and recommend larger studies.

F.1.2 Practice Effect Confound in H6 (Deskilling Resistance)

· Description: The AI-free retention test at Session 6 is confounded by practice effects; teams may improve due to repeated exposure to the simulation, masking deskilling.
· Fix: Add a control condition (Human-only teams with no AI exposure across all sessions) as Study 3. Compare the change in AI-free performance between HCSA+AI teams with and without anti-deskilling protocols, and the Human-only control. This isolates the deskilling effect.

F.1.3 Pilot Study Circularity in Ground Truth Generation

· Description: Stage 4 of ground truth generation uses pilot team discovery rates to filter problems. If pilot teams are predominantly undifferentiated, problems only discoverable by HCSA teams might be eliminated as "too hard".
· Fix: Use half HCSA and half undifferentiated pilot teams (as already specified in v0.5). Additionally, in Stage 5, if a problem is discovered by HCSA but not undiff, retain it regardless of the overall 20–80% rule, as it is a high-value differentiator. This avoids circularity.

F.2 Medium Severity

F.2.1 Undifferentiated Baseline Weakness

· Description: The undifferentiated condition may not be a strong control because real teams spontaneously differentiate. Without explicit role assignment, they may still develop informal specialization, reducing the contrast.
· Fix: Include a third human condition with partial role assignment (e.g., two roles) to test whether the benefit is due to the number of roles or the specific four-role structure. Alternatively, measure role drift and use it as a covariate or mediator. This is partially addressed by RoleDrift measure but not explicitly analyzed.

F.2.2 GRA Scope Practicality

· Description: The GRA's responsibilities span multiple disciplines (ethics, psychology, law, governance). A single individual may lack depth in all areas.
· Fix: Acknowledge this limitation more explicitly and consider allowing the GRA to consult an external knowledge base (AI retrieval) more heavily. H10 tests split-role, but the practical feasibility of a single GRA remains questionable. Suggest that in operational settings, the GRA role might be filled by a small team, but for the experiment a single agent is used for control.

F.2.3 AI Tool Capability Level Unspecified

· Description: The AI tools' absolute performance is not specified. If the AI is very weak, H2 is trivially true; if very strong, H2 is harder to test.
· Fix: Pre-register that the AI tools' standalone HPD-$F_1$ will be reported in Study 1. If AI-only performance is <10% of ground truth, H2 may be deemed uninformative. Alternatively, include a "strong AI" condition with a more capable LLM to ensure a meaningful baseline.

F.2.4 Time Pressure Floor Effect

· Description: The 30-minute scenario may produce floor effects (HPD-$F_1$ <0.10 for all conditions), making H8 untestable.
· Fix: Pre-register a floor effect criterion: if 30-min scenarios yield pooled HPD-$F_1$ < 0.10, H8 is deferred and 45-min becomes the minimum time pressure. This is already partially addressed in Section 5.3.

F.3 Low Severity

F.3.1 Terminology: "Proposition" vs "Theorem"

· Description: Proposition 2.1 is labeled a proposition but still uses proof-style language. Some may still treat it as a theorem.
· Fix: Emphasize in text that it is a design hypothesis formalized mathematically, not a claim about the universe. The proof demonstrates internal consistency, not empirical truth.

F.3.2 Citation Style Inconsistency

· Description: Some references lack full details (e.g., "Do et al., 2016" without journal).
· Fix: Ensure all citations include DOI or NTRS ID as planned.

F.3.3 Novel Discovery Weight $w_N$ Arbitrary

· Description: The weight $w_N=0.5$ in HPD-$F_1$ is arbitrary.
· Fix: Pre-register sensitivity analyses with $w_N \in \{0.25, 0.5, 0.75\}$ and report all. If results change qualitatively, discuss implications.

F.3.4 AI-Only Multi-Agent Configuration Underspecified

· Description: AI-M (multiple homogeneous AI agents) lacks detail on how they coordinate.
· Fix: Specify that AI-M consists of 4 identical AI agents running in parallel, each with access to the full tool suite, and they share a common memory/workspace. They may specialize via self-organization, but this is not scripted. This makes AI-M a fair comparison to HCSA.

